  "worked_solution": "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{\u2032}(x)={g}^{\u2032}(x)-{f}^{\u2032}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{\u2032}(2)={g}^{\u2032}(2)-{f}^{\u2032}(2).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=2$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-1,3)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-1,3)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934289/assets/814ce0fdce1428b3bd619caa5e8d09a828d2f2c52fd0b20b7f520562319fb848.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-1,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u0394f}{\u0394x}=\\frac{4-1}{3-(-1)}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{\u2032}(2)=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-1,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u0394g}{\u0394x}=\\frac{1-4}{3-(-1)}=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{\u2032}(2)=-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{\u2032}(2) & ={g}^{\u2032}(2)-{f}^{\u2032}(2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
